change(function(){location.href='/results/' + $('#other_games_select').val();})"]] " | " [:a {:href "/event_names/379"} "Event type"]] [:h1 {:class "event_title"} "Double Sculls, Men" [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "14 – 21 August 2004"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/788"} "Olympiako Kopelatodramio Skhoinia, Schinias National Park"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/159743"} "28 from 14 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The reigning Olympic champions from Slovenia, " [:a {:href "/athletes/39682"} "Luka Špik"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/39677"} "Iztok Čop"] ", travelled to the 2004 Summer Olympics to defend their crown in the men’s double sculls, but they would not go unchallenged, as the reigning World Champions and 2001 runners-up, " [:a {:href "/athletes/37124"} "Sébastien Vielledent"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92546"} "Adrien Hardy"] " of France, also had a strong claim to the title. The French duo had also been the winners of the 2002 Double Sculls Challenge Cup at the Henley Royal Regatta. Hungarians " [:a {:href "/athletes/92596"} "Ákos Haller"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92597"} "Tibor Pető"] " were the 2001 and 2002 World Champions, but Haller had a different partner, " [:a {:href "/athletes/104328"} "Gábor Bencsik"] " in Athens. The Italians had been Olympic and World bronze medalists in 2000 and 2001 respectively and had been runner-up at the last two Worlds, most recently with their Olympic crew of " [:a {:href "/athletes/38378"} "Rossano Galtarossa"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92616"} "Alessio Sartori"] ", both of whom were Olympic champions in the quadruple sculls from 2000. Cuba sent " [:a {:href "/athletes/92516"} "Yosbel Martínez"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/92514"} "Yoennis Hernández"] ", the reigning Pan-American champions."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Italy, Slovenia, and France won their opening heats, all in times significantly ahead of the rest of the field, while Hungary won the repêchage. The semi-finals were won by Slovenia and Italy, the latter of whom set an Olympic record and defeated France in the process. Hungary and Cuba, meanwhile, were eliminated in Slovenia’s heat. The final was fairly spread out, with France, Slovenia, and Italy taking gold, silver, and bronze without much of a challenge and comfortably ahead of the rest of the pack."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Pair"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/37124"} "Sébastien Vieilledent"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/92546"} "Adrien Hardy"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/FRA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/FRA.png"}] "FRA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/39682"} "Luka Špik"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/39677"} "Iztok Čop"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SLO"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/SLO.png"}] "SLO"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/38378"} "Rossano Galtarossa"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/92616"} "Alessio Sartori"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/92536"} "Leonid Gulov"] " / " [:a {:href "/athletes/104330"} "Tõnu Endrekson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/EST"} [:img 